# f1 = add, f2 = sub, f3 = low, f5 = overVariable
# Two closures were seen in a module variable. A hoisted check tests the variable for those two, before the loop that calls it.
# Compiled code tests "one of these functions" by runs of closure numbers: not below the first of a run, not above its last.
# RUN(SET): walk f5 mechanisms=all
# SET: = TypeTest {{*}}testedType=f1|f2
# With --aot-harden=proof-checks (testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime) the value that has passed the test is checked for the same type. That check compares with each function by itself and with no run.
# RUN(CHECKED): walk f5 mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime
# CHECKED: = TypeTest {{*}}testedType=f1|f2
# CHECKED: = CheckProof {{*}}checkedType=f1|f2 {{*}}source=Test
# The shell calls the function that contains the loop, stores a third function into the variable out of the compiler's sight, and calls the function again.
# RUN(RIGHT): run-in-shell testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime flag = function () { return true; }; outside = function (lay, first, second, third) { lay(third); }; drive = function (over) { print(over(1), over(3)); };
# RIGHT: 11 34
# RIGHT-NEXT: 7 16
# RIGHT-NEXT: exited with 0
# Fault injection, testing=OverstateClosureNumberRuns: the last number of a run is taken one higher, so the function that is declared right after the two passes the test.
# RUN(RUNS): bytes f5:Body mechanisms=all testing=OverstateClosureNumberRuns
# RUNS: relocation {{*}}ClosureFunction {{*}}"sub" plus 1
# By itself the fault changes no result here. After the test for the two, the branch that is inlined tests for its own function, and whatever else has passed runs the loop as written.
# RUN(ALONE): run-in-shell testing=OverstateClosureNumberRuns flag = function () { return true; }; outside = function (lay, first, second, third) { lay(third); }; drive = function (over) { print(over(1), over(3)); };
# ALONE: 11 34
# ALONE-NEXT: 7 16
# ALONE-NEXT: exited with 0
